00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:15,840
see what they can come up with. So ideally, what will happen is you'll go through a couple of

197
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:21,120
campouts like that at first, and they will go right to the handbook. when a question comes up,

198
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:24,879
They'll put their heads together and start solving the problems before they even come to

199
00:19:24,880 --> 00:19:30,720
you, before you even have to start asking questions. and that's exactly what we want them to do.

200
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:36,720
With younger guys, you're going to be planning and leading more now than you will a year from now.

201
00:19:36,720 --> 00:19:42,080
You'll have to model what a patrol leader does and then hand those responsibilities off a little

202
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:49,280
as time as they grow capable of taking them on. The key is putting the initiative on your Scouts.

203
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:53,680
Guide them to the answers they need, Don't just tell them what to do next.

204
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:57,920
They'll catch on pretty fast and a year or two things will be very different

205
00:19:57,920 --> 00:20:02,080
And as the younger Scouts are coming in, these Scouts will be a couple of years older.

206
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:06,320
They'll be very familiar with how to discover things and how to make things happen.

207
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:09,920
They're naturally going to pick up some leadership skills along the way.

208
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:13,200
They're going to naturally pick up some planning skills along the way,

209
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:17,840
But for those first few months, what's vitally important is giving them plenty

210
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:21,520
of opportunity to do the things that Scouts do. Don't make them plan it,

211
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:24,560
Don't make them try and come up with it, Just give them the opportunity.

212
00:20:25,120 --> 00:20:30,480
Then get them in the process of discovering how to do these things

213
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:35,760
And you're kind of setting an example for the future in how everything's going to happen.

214
00:20:37,280 --> 00:20:42,560
Brian Snyder is an assistant scout master with Troop 91 in St Paul, Minnesota,

215
00:20:42,560 --> 00:20:45,440
And he writes to say thanks for all you do for scouting.

216
00:20:45,440 --> 00:20:47,200
I hope you don't get tired of hearing that

217
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:50,160
You're a magnificent source of knowledge and inspiration.

218
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:54,160
Well, thank you, Brian. I don't get tired of hearing that, Thank you.

219
00:20:55,520 --> 00:20:58,880
I've recently turned to Scouts because I have three sons,

220
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:04,160
Two Boy Scouts and one Cub Scout, who keep me busy as an assistant scout master and den leader.

221
00:21:04,880 --> 00:21:10,400
Your recent series on the patrol method prompts some questions on starting or restarting

222
00:21:10,400 --> 00:21:13,040
and reviving the patrol method. in our Troop

223
00:21:13,040 --> 00:21:15,440
We pride ourselves on being youth led.

224
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:19,200
However, I feel we're failing working on the patrol method.

225
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:22,960
We have around two dozen boys and three patrols. at the moment.

226
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:26,560
There's not much intermingling between the older Scouts and the younger Scouts,

227
00:21:26,560 --> 00:21:29,520
which leads to a little animosity from the younger Scouts-

228
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:33,200
I'm being barked at by the older Scouts- and some aloofness from the older Scouts

229
00:21:33,200 --> 00:21:35,840
who don't want to play with the little kids. quote unquote:

230
00:21:35,840 --> 00:21:40,240
I understand the pitfalls of having the adults dictate who's going to be in what patrol,

231
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:45,280
But I don't know how to encourage the boys to take a critical look at the patrol method

232
00:21:45,280 --> 00:21:48,240
and how the patrol method will help them get the most out of scouting.

233
00:21:48,240 --> 00:21:50,720
They seem to be happy with the status quo.

234
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:56,160
It seems to me that we subscribe more to the idea that patrols are just kind of an administrative way

235
00:21:56,160 --> 00:21:59,440
to break up the boys in the Troop into manageable pieces,

236
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:04,240
instead of the patrols being actual vital functional units of scouting.

237
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:07,120
The patrol leaders council for the most part follows the script

238
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:11,040
and tends to fill in the blanks on the calendar with the same events and campouts.

239
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:14,160
I'm sure he challenges them to think outside the box, though

240
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:18,320
I, on the other hand, am the type of person who constantly asks

241
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:22,480
why do we do it this way and feel that because it's always been done this way

242
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:23,920
is not a good enough answer.

243
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:27,920
There's a younger scout patrol and most of them have been together since Tiger Cubs,

244
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:29,200
so they're pretty tight.

245
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:33,920
The next patrol is a little older and has also been together since Tigers,

246
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:36,880
and there's the third patrol is all older Scouts.

247
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:41,200
Again, it seems to me that we should change things around

248
00:22:41,200 --> 00:22:44,480
and the older boys should be distributed with the younger.

249
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:47,920
I wholly embrace that. this needs to come from the Scouts,

250
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:52,160
but with the help of the adults and not the adults regardless of what they think.

251
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:55,600
Well, Brian, I've got a few things that may be helpful for you.

252
00:22:55,600 --> 00:22:59,120
I'll say first, it's important to understand that the scout master is the adult in

253
00:22:59,120 --> 00:23:03,920
charge of the adults and is the adult position empowered to make meaningful changes.

254
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:06,560
Instead of looking at the way your patrols are made up now

255
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:09,680
as being the source of the frustration you may be feeling,

256
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:12,320
or you may be seeing a different way to rearrange it,

257
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:14,240
Let's look at larger issues.

258
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:18,960
Let's say you went ahead and made the changes that you would like to make in the patrols.

259
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:21,840
You mixed up the older and the younger boys and everything like that.

260
00:23:21,840 --> 00:23:24,160
Well, just shuffling around isn't going to help.

261
00:23:24,160 --> 00:23:29,200
You can't affect this sort of thing by reorganizing the patrol chart.

262
00:23:29,200 --> 00:23:32,000
If there's hard feelings between older Scouts and younger Scouts,

263
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:33,840
older Scouts are barking at younger Scouts.

264
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:36,560
you know. rearranging the chart is not going to help

265
00:23:37,360 --> 00:23:40,080
The fundamental way that youth leadership develops

266
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:44,800
and the way Scouts experience the program is really what you want to be aiming at?

267
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:50,720
The answer always comes back to Scouts having real responsibility for their program.

268
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:53,919
That's the real focus that gets things moving

269
00:23:53,919 --> 00:23:57,679
and starts to solve some of the difficulties that you're looking at.

270
00:23:57,679 --> 00:24:00,639
If you have this kind of antagonism going on,

271
00:24:00,639 --> 00:24:03,600
there's something missing in the attitude of the older Scouts

272
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:05,360
that you need to be paying attention to,

273
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:10,320
Their ideal of service and their following. the Scout oath and law

274
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:12,160
probably needs a little bit of work.

275
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:14,480
Once older Scouts are instilled with that,

276
00:24:14,480 --> 00:24:16,560
they stop barking at younger Scouts,

277
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:19,440
They stop being antagonistic towards them.

278
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:22,560
I had this letter from. I had this email from Bill.

279
00:24:22,560 --> 00:24:26,400
He says I'm copying my assistant scout masters, hoping you'll reply, all

280
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:29,920
so that we are all hearing the same thing when we follow up

281
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:32,560
and discuss this amongst ourselves.

282
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:34,960
I've been listening to your podcasts and reading your blog posts

283
00:24:34,960 --> 00:24:37,200
and other materials the last few months

284
00:24:37,200 --> 00:24:39,520
and it's become one of my favorite pastimes,

285
00:24:39,520 --> 00:24:42,080
and I just finished reading your book, The Scouting Journey,

286
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:43,040
and I really liked it.

287
00:24:43,680 --> 00:24:46,640
I took over as scout master last spring.

288
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:48,400
We elected a senior patrol leader

289
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:51,840
and I pretty much spoon fed that senior patrol leader everything.

290
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:53,200
He had some ideas of his own

291
00:24:53,200 --> 00:24:54,880
and we certainly let him run with those.

292
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:57,600
However, looking back he was more like a puppet

293
00:24:57,600 --> 00:24:58,960
doing what I told him to do.

294
00:24:59,520 --> 00:25:02,480
Our new senior patrol leader is much more independent

295
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:05,920
and our last camp out was much more scout led

296
00:25:05,920 --> 00:25:08,960
but also a little bit less organized.

297
00:25:08,960 --> 00:25:10,480
Our new senior patrol leader announced

298
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:12,400
he was going to have a patrol leaders council meeting

299
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:13,200
at his home.

300
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:14,960
He didn't invite any of the adults,

301
00:25:14,960 --> 00:25:18,320
but the next day he emailed me an agenda for the troop meeting

302
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:21,360
with all the assignments listed and a pretty good plan.

303
00:25:21,360 --> 00:25:24,560
At the troop meeting all the Scouts forgot their assignments,

304
00:25:24,560 --> 00:25:26,240
but they managed to improvise

305
00:25:26,240 --> 00:25:27,920
and everybody seemed to have fun,

306
00:25:27,920 --> 00:25:30,560
even though the meeting turned out to be a little chaotic.

307
00:25:30,560 --> 00:25:31,840
During the portion of the meeting

308
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:33,520
we would usually hold patrol meetings,

309
00:25:33,520 --> 00:25:35,520
The senior patrol leader announced

310
00:25:35,520 --> 00:25:37,280
we were going to reorganize patrols,

311
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:39,760
with no prior warning or notice to me.

312
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:42,400
They were going to elect patrol leaders first

313
00:25:42,400 --> 00:25:45,520
and then the Scouts would choose the patrol they wanted to be in.

314
00:25:45,520 --> 00:25:47,200
The way he ran the election was something

315
00:25:47,200 --> 00:25:48,240
I'd never seen before.

316
00:25:48,240 --> 00:25:50,000
He called for nominations,

317
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,800
had five nominees standing in front of the troop

318
00:25:52,800 --> 00:25:54,560
and had a series of votes

319
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:57,760
to narrow it down to two patrol leaders.

320
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:00,240
At that point the Scouts excitedly ran over

321
00:26:00,240 --> 00:26:04,160
to the patrol leader that they wanted to be in a patrol, with

322
00:26:04,160 --> 00:26:05,760
A few of them shifted back and forth

323
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:08,480
before settling on which patrol they wanted to be in,

324
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:10,000
and to my amazement-

325
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,720
and, I know, to the amazement of my assistant scoutmasters-

326
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:15,840
the patrols ended up being evenly balanced

327
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:18,240
in both numbers, age and experience.

328
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:20,880
I was really worried about hurt feelings,

329
00:26:20,880 --> 00:26:23,360
but there was a lot of positive energy in the room

330
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:25,280
and I didn't want to stop what was happening.

331
00:26:25,280 --> 00:26:26,800
I felt a mixture of excitement

332
00:26:26,800 --> 00:26:29,840
and seeing the senior patrol leader take over the troop,

333
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:32,640
but worried that he may be creating problems.

334
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:35,760
In the end, the whole process of electing patrol leaders

335
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:38,240
and setting up new patrols was over in about 10 minutes.

336
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:39,840
It was absolutely amazing.

337
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:42,160
Then they went right into picking their patrol names

338
00:26:42,160 --> 00:26:44,400
and there was lots of excitement and noise.

339
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:47,040
When the youth leaders reviewed that troop meeting,

340
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:49,360
they were generally more critical of themselves

341
00:26:49,360 --> 00:26:51,280
than I believe that the Scouts would be

342
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:53,600
Now for the question.

343
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:56,400
If left to themselves, I believe our troop meetings and campouts

344
00:26:56,400 --> 00:26:58,640
will definitely be less organized

345
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:00,800
and doing less learning of skills

346
00:27:00,800 --> 00:27:05,040
than if the adult scouters were involved in making the plans.

347
00:27:05,680 --> 00:27:09,280
Personally, I get the feeling that we should go as far as possible,

348
00:27:09,280 --> 00:27:11,280
letting the Scouts run their troop.

349
00:27:11,280 --> 00:27:13,600
but I also wonder how much direction and training

350
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:15,600
we should be giving in what form?

351
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:18,960
Well, hello, Bill and Bill's assistant scoutmasters

352
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:22,080
I got to tell you it sounds like to me

353
00:27:22,800 --> 00:27:25,520
things are going really, really well.

354
00:27:25,520 --> 00:27:29,040
I understand that you may be both amazed

355
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:30,879
and concerned at the same time,

356
00:27:30,879 --> 00:27:32,639
but you've got a senior patrol leader

357
00:27:32,639 --> 00:27:35,120
who seems to understand what he wants to do

358
00:27:35,120 --> 00:27:36,720
and so far seems to have come up

359
00:27:36,720 --> 00:27:39,360
with really good strategies on making that happen.

360
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:42,960
So let's take a new perspective on what's going on.

361
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:45,360
You're saying, hey, you know, if we're not involved,

362
00:27:45,360 --> 00:27:46,800
they're not going to be doing scout skills

363
00:27:46,800 --> 00:27:47,920
and everything like that.

364
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:50,560
but what you described to me in that meeting

365
00:27:50,560 --> 00:27:52,960
is exactly what we want to see happening in Scouts.

366
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:54,960
That's real scouting.

367
00:27:54,960 --> 00:27:57,120
That's Scouts doing what Scouts do.

368
00:27:57,120 --> 00:27:59,360
They've taken their fate in their own hands.

369
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:00,480
They've made some decisions.

370
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:02,640
They're figuring things out on their own.

371
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:05,920
That's what we really, really want to see happening.

372
00:28:05,920 --> 00:28:08,640
So let's take that perspective first.

373
00:28:08,640 --> 00:28:10,400
If you think about what they're getting

374
00:28:10,400 --> 00:28:12,400
out of their participation in the activities

375
00:28:12,400 --> 00:28:13,440
as you describe them

376
00:28:14,160 --> 00:28:16,880
you know, look past the requirements and the skills

377
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:21,360
to what specific areas of character are being developed.

378
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:24,000
Do you see the ideals of the scout oath and law

379
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:25,280
being practiced

380
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:28,400
and being active in what the Scouts are doing.

381
00:28:28,400 --> 00:28:29,760
I certainly do

382
00:28:29,760 --> 00:28:32,080
You express some concern that they'll drift away

383
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:34,160
from the broader context of why they're Scouts,

384
00:28:34,160 --> 00:28:35,600
and this is a common concern.

385
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:39,600
but I talk about doing what Scouts do

386
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:42,800
as the context or the rules of the game.

387
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:44,240
Now we don't want to take all those

388
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:46,160
wonderful, active, vital things

389
00:28:46,160 --> 00:28:48,560
and just fragment them into a list of requirements

390
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:50,560
that adults teach to Scouts.

391
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:55,600
No, we don't want that kind of disconnected deal going on.

392
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:57,280
Remember Baden Powell said that-

393
00:28:57,280 --> 00:29:00,159
that the acquisition of skills in scouting

394
00:29:00,159 --> 00:29:02,159
is like getting a suntan:

395
00:29:02,159 --> 00:29:05,040
a natural result of having fun in the outdoors.

396
00:29:05,040 --> 00:29:07,600
Your Scouts will need some help here and there

397
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:09,760
to understand the game

398
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:11,200
and to stay in context,

399
00:29:11,840 --> 00:29:13,679
but a wise scoutmaster

400
00:29:13,679 --> 00:29:15,520
and wise assistant scoutmasters

401
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:17,200
will be observing

402
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:20,240
and be encouraging them in the right directions.

403
00:29:20,240 --> 00:29:22,880
And I think what's really most important

404
00:29:22,880 --> 00:29:25,200
about what the adults involved

405
00:29:25,200 --> 00:29:26,880
in this whole thing are doing

406
00:29:26,880 --> 00:29:30,240
is that you have the single most important attribute

407
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:33,120
to making yourselves into great Scouts

408
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:35,360
and that is the desire to study

409
00:29:35,360 --> 00:29:36,720
and learn your work,

410
00:29:36,720 --> 00:29:38,560
to examine, to question.

411
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:41,280
You're aware that you might get things wrong,

412
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:42,160
which seems simple,

413
00:29:42,160 --> 00:29:43,920
but a lot of Scouts seem to accept

414
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:45,280
the idea academically

415
00:29:45,280 --> 00:29:46,880
that they might be wrong,

416
00:29:46,880 --> 00:29:48,720
but then they don't want to do the heavy lifting

417
00:29:48,720 --> 00:29:50,080
of changing what they do.

418
00:29:52,880 --> 00:29:54,080
I think it's great.

419
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:56,960
I think what's going on is great

420
00:29:56,960 --> 00:29:59,200
and I think also, at the same time,

421
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:02,560
every Scouter should be slightly dissatisfied

422
00:30:02,560 --> 00:30:04,240
with ourselves as Scouter

423
00:30:04,240 --> 00:30:07,520
and seek to do the best work possible,

424
00:30:07,520 --> 00:30:08,960
And a lot of times

425
00:30:08,960 --> 00:30:10,560
that work is observing

426
00:30:10,560 --> 00:30:11,680
and asking questions

427
00:30:11,680 --> 00:30:14,000
and giving hints and ideas

428
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:15,360
and directions

429
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:18,480
to Scouts on a much less active basis

430
00:30:18,480 --> 00:30:21,120
than we first think we're going to be doing

431
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:23,040
Now. right now you've got a Senior Petroleum

432
00:30:23,040 --> 00:30:24,880
with tons of initiative,

433
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:26,240
who's got great strategies,

434
00:30:26,240 --> 00:30:27,280
is really working hard

435
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:28,720
to make things happen.

436
00:30:28,720 --> 00:30:30,400
That's fantastic.

437
00:30:30,400 --> 00:30:32,080
So respond to that

438
00:30:32,080 --> 00:30:33,600
by backing away,

439
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:35,760
helping him stay in context,

440
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:37,520
asking him questions,

441
00:30:37,520 --> 00:30:39,120
being there to help him out

442
00:30:39,120 --> 00:30:40,560
when he needs help

443
00:30:40,560 --> 00:30:42,880
and accepting that things

444
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:44,080
may be a little chaotic.

445
00:30:44,080 --> 00:30:46,240
they may not be as nice and neat

446
00:30:46,240 --> 00:30:47,680
if adults were doing everything,

447
00:30:48,320 --> 00:30:50,640
but to me you're really hitting on all

